{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q016_pdb_1acc_domain_split","answer":"PDB 1acc chain A is the anthrax protective antigen. SCOP 1.75 represented it as a single composite domain, d1acca_, in the Anthrax protective antigen family and superfamily, b.87.1.1 and b.87.1. Later SCOPe curation split that whole-chain assignment into separate domain entries. I cannot reliably specify the exact replacement residue ranges, domain count, or first release containing the revision from pretrained knowledge alone.

The important change is the unit of evolutionary classification: a multidomain protein previously represented by one entry was decomposed into smaller structural modules. This is a revision of the annotation, not a change in the protein. Domains can have distinct evolutionary histories and occur in different protein architectures; their boundaries are hypotheses that can improve as structural and comparative evidence accumulates. A split does not, by itself, demonstrate that every resulting domain folds independently.

Homology searches should therefore use the revised domain sequences and boundaries, inspect domain-level alignment coverage, and record the classification release. A match to one domain should not be extended automatically to the entire protective antigen or its other domains. Family membership supports a closer evolutionary relationship; superfamily membership supports broader common ancestry. Shared fold or class alone does not establish homology. Likewise, a domain match alone cannot justify transferring toxin activity, receptor specificity, or the entire chain as a modeling template; those require appropriate functional, sequence, and structural evidence.","evidence_used":["pretrained model knowledge"]}]}
